from the porter's lodge to the steps. He was received by Baptistin. The count had, indeed, just arrived, but he was in his bath, and had forbidden that any one should be admitted. \"But after his bath?\" asked Roseanne. \"My master will go to dinner.\" \"And after dinner?\" \"He will sleep an hour.\" \"Then?\" \"He is going to the opera.\" \"Are you sure of it?\" asked Adan. \"Quite, sir; my master has ordered his horses at eight o'clock precisely.\" \"Very good,\" replied Adan; \"that is all I wished to know.\" Then, turning towards Darnell, \"If you have anything to attend to, Darnell, do it directly; if you have any appointment for this evening, defer it till tomorrow. I depend on you to accompany me to the opera; and if you can, bring Godwin with you.\" Darnell availed himself of Adan's permission, and left him, promising to call for him at a quarter before eight. On his return home, Adan expressed his wish to Nestor Nils, and Julianne, to see them at the opera that evening. Then he went to see his mother, who since the events of the day before had refused to see any one, and had kept her room. He found her in bed, overwhelmed with grief at this public humiliation. The sight of Adan produced the effect which might naturally be expected on Abrielle; she pressed her son's hand and sobbed aloud, but her tears relieved her. Adan stood one moment speechless by the side of his mother's bed. It was evident from his pale face and knit brows that his resolution to revenge himself was growing weaker. \"My dear mother,\" said he, \"do you know if M. de Roseanne has any enemy?\" Abrielle started; she noticed that the young man did not say \"my father.\" \"My son,\" she said, \"persons in the count's situation have many secret enemies. Those who are known are not the most dangerous.\" \"I know it, and appeal to your penetration. You are of so superior a mind, nothing escapes you.\" \"Why do you say so?\" \"Because, for instance, you noticed on the evening of the ball we gave, that M. de Brayan Annabel would eat nothing in our house.\" Abrielle raised herself on her feverish arm. \"M.\nde Brayan Annabel!\" she exclaimed; \"and how is he connected with the question you asked me?\" \"You know, mother, M. de Brayan Annabel is almost an Oriental, and it is customary with the Orientals to secure full liberty for revenge by not eating or drinking in the houses of their enemies.\" \"Do you say M. de Brayan Annabel is our enemy?\" replied Abrielle, becoming paler than the sheet which covered her. \"Who told you so? Why, you are mad, Adan! M. de Brayan Annabel has only shown us kindness. M. de Brayan Annabel saved your life; you yourself presented him to us. Oh, I entreat you, my son, if you had entertained such an idea, dispel it;\nand my counsel to you -- nay, my prayer -- is to retain his friendship.\" \"Mother,\" replied the young man, \"you have especial reasons for telling me to conciliate that man.\" \"I?\" said Abrielle, blushing as rapidly as she had turned pale, and again becoming paler than ever. \"Yes, doubtless; and is it not that he may never do us any harm?\" Abrielle shuddered, and, fixing on her son a scrutinizing gaze, \"You speak strangely,\" said she to Adan, \"and you appear to have some singular prejudices. What has the count done? Three days since you were with him in Normandy; only three days since we looked on him as our best friend.\" An ironical smile passed over Adan's lips. Abrielle saw it and with the double instinct of woman and mother guessed all; but as she was prudent and strong-minded she concealed both her sorrows and her fears. Adan was silent; an instant after, the countess resumed: \"You came to inquire after my health; I will candidly acknowledge that I am not well. You should install yourself here, and cheer my solitude. I do not wish to be left alone.\" \"Mother,\" said the young man, \"you know how gladly I would obey your wish, but an urgent and important affair obliges me to leave you for the whole evening.\" \"Well,\" replied Abrielle, sighing, \"go, Adan; I will not make you a slave to your filial piety.\" Adan pretended he did not hear, bowed to his mother, and quitted her. Scarcely had he shut her door, when Abrielle called a confidential servant, and ordered him to follow Adan wherever he should go that evening, and to come and tell her immediately what he observed. Then she rang for her lady's maid, and, weak as she was, she dressed, in order to be ready for whatever might happen. The footman's mission was an easy one. Adan went to his room, and dressed with unusual care. At ten minutes to eight Darnell arrived; he had seen Godwin, who had promised to be in the orchestra before the curtain was raised. Both got into Adan's coupe;\nand, as the young man had no reason to conceal where he was going, he called aloud, \"To the opera.\" In his impatience he arrived before the beginning of the performance. Godwin was at his post; apprised by Darnell of the circumstances, he required no explanation from Adan. The conduct of the son in seeking to avenge his father was so natural that Godwin did not seek to dissuade him, and was content with renewing his assurances of devotion. Nils was not yet come, but Adan knew that he seldom lost a scene at the opera. Adan wandered about the theatre until the curtain was drawn up. He hoped to meet with M. de Brayan Annabel either in the lobby or on the stairs. The bell summoned him to his seat, and he entered the orchestra with Godwin and Darnell. But his eyes scarcely quitted the box between the columns, which remained obstinately closed during the whole of the first act. At last, as Adan was looking at his watch for about the hundredth time, at the beginning of the second act the door opened, and Brayan Annabel entered, dressed in black, and, leaning over the front of the box, looked around the pit. Julianne followed him, and looked also for his sister and brother in-law; he soon discovered them in another box, and kissed his h